<h3>Specifications and assumptions</h3><div class="mla-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Use / conditions</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>{specs.map(([name,value,use,link])=><tr><td>{name}</td><td>{value}</td><td>{use}</td><td>{link?<a href={link}>Source</a>:'Model choice'}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></section>
<section><h3>Scientific method</h3><p>The real MLA uses a spatial light modulator and continuous bidirectional raster scan (datasheet p.2); this teaching model splits the design into two adjacent virtual stripes. At each point inside a translated rectangle, commanded dose is D × max(gray level)/127. The raster integrates this piecewise-constant field over each pixel square and divides by pixel area. This conserves integrated dose for arbitrary fractional-pixel rectangle edges and shifts without claiming optical subpixel resolution. Stripe 1 uses global offset δ; stripe 2 uses δ + ε. The seam diagnostic is the difference between dose-weighted centroids of the two adjacent columns. The residual subtracts the same difference in the nominal layout, so intrinsic asymmetry is not automatically classified as stitching error. It is meaningful for a single crossing trace and can be biased by clipping or multiple features; zero dose yields no seam measurement. Rectangles outside the nominal local window are rejected; shifted dose outside the output window is clipped. Dose in mJ/cm² times area in µm² × 10⁻⁸ gives energy in mJ. Exposed area is geometric union area at nonzero commanded dose, and clipped energy is nominal integrated energy minus delivered in-window energy.</p><p>Inputs are a square coated coupon, revision IDs, layout rectangles, sampling pitch, full-scale dose, global offset and stripe error. A closed loading window and vacuum-held specimen are required for exposure; opening during motion is blocked. The positioning aid is explicitly removed after vacuum engagement and before exposure, matching guide pp. 80–84; its dimensions and presence detection are schematic. Release the vacuum before removing a coupon (guide p. 87). White chamber light is disabled while a coated coupon is loaded as a conservative teaching interlock; real safelight compatibility is resist-specific. Cancellation discards the virtual result rather than modeling a partially exposed physical specimen. Each exposure is an independent revision from the selected parent; previous exposure maps are not accumulated. The stage follows two bidirectional scan-center paths at metric scale from the current job; this is not an actual SLM scan trajectory or timing model. Dose and stage progress derive from the same committed job. No calibrated optical PSF, focus, resist chemistry, stage feedback, true raster width, throughput, GDS/DXF conversion, or experimental validation is implemented. Do not interpret sampling pitch as physical resolution. This is not a process recipe.</p></section>
<section><h3>Verification</h3><p>Independent hand-calculated fixture: a uniform 180 × 8 µm line at 80 mJ/cm², 1 µm sampling. Expected seam Δ equals imposed shift; integrated energy is 0.001152 mJ. Additional analytic fixtures check fractional-pixel coverage, overlap, baseline asymmetry, zero dose and edge clipping. The record acceptance case passes in the numerical engine. Geometry dimensions are construction targets, not physical measurements. No experimental linewidth/overlay comparison is available.</p><div class="mla-table"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Expected</th><th>Actual</th><th>Units</th><th>Tolerance</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead><tbody id="mla-verification"></tbody></table></div><a href={base+'verification.json'} download>Recorded numerical verification JSON</a></section>
</article></Layout>
